# Normalize line endings so the framework reads the same on every platform.
#
# This is not cosmetic. The framework's own drift table records a real incident
# caused by line endings: a coverage floor was calibrated from a CRLF checkout
# and went red on CI's LF checkout, because the same source produced a different
# number. The check-progress hook (`.claude/hooks/check-progress.ps1`) compares a
# SHA read from a page against `git rev-parse`; anything that makes two machines
# disagree about the same file is a bug waiting to happen in a framework whose
# first discipline is "read it, do not infer it".
#
# Nothing here is about how the files are *edited* — editors may still write CRLF.
# Git normalizes on commit and checkout.

* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell hooks are executed by Windows PowerShell, which is tolerant of LF.
# Forcing LF keeps the file diffable against a Linux checkout.
*.ps1   text eol=lf
# CRLF breaks the shebang on Linux.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf

# Keep the architecture diagram binary — never let git touch it.
*.png   binary
